ich means that it is even.\n\nNow, we consider the case when $m$ and $n$ are both odd.\n\n> Case 2. If $m$ and $n$ are odd, then $m=2a+1$ and $n=2b+1$ for some integers $a$ and $b$.\n> As a result, we obtain\n> $3m - 7n + 2|= 3(2a + 1) - 7(2b + 1) + 2; = 6a + 3 - 14b - 7 + 2; = 6a - 14b - 2; = 2(3a - 7b - 1)$.\n> Let $c=3a-7b-1$. Since $a$ and $b$ are integers, we have that $c$ is an integer.\n> So, $3m-7n+2 = 2c$, which means that it is even.\n\nFinally, we combine both cases:\n\n> Combining the cases above, we can conclude that $3m-7n+2$ is even for any integers $m$ and $n$ of the same parity." 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198211 1069 "e-13320" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198211 1071 "Sketch a representation of the set $\\,\\displaystyle \\bigcup_{i \\in I} X_i$ in the Cartesian plane given that $I = [-1,1] \\subset \\mathbb{R}$ and $X_i = \\{i \\} \\times [0, \\, i^2]$." 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198211 1072 #uuid "aa337681-5d8c-5ea1-b0fe-5d8cd15040a7" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198211 1074 "Recall that the union of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *at least one* set:\n\n$$\n\\bigcup_{i \\in I} A_i = \\big\\{x \\:: \\: x \\in A_k \\:\\:\\textrm{for at least one}\\:\\: k \\in I \\big\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=-0.5$, we have\n\n$$\nX_{-0.5} = \\{-0.5 \\} \\times [0, 0.25] = \\big\\{(-0.5,k) \\:: \\: k \\in [0, 0.25] \\big\\}\n$$\n\nThis represents a vertical line segment from the point $(-0.5, 0)$ to the point $(-0.5,0.25)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/cc/ccc75eac899efcab1d0b6fde12609a3ea8f61d6fec74c739ef3393fad32645a9.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\nX_i = \\{i \\} \\times [0, i^2] = \\big\\{(i,k) \\:: \\: k \\in [0, i^2] \\big\\}\n$$\n\nis the vertical line segment from the point $(i,0)$ to the point $(i, i^2)$.\n\n![](images/60/60c98be5d97748e9285bb5e9c30bba9781a5ef99245789718a7828b18a6df8fb.png)\n\nAs $i$ runs over $I$, these line segments sweep the following figure that represents the union $\\displaystyle \\bigcup_{i \\in I} X_i{:}$\n\n![](images/b3/b3a61d686c78c76a4f461c1c771437d31030339b634661da8b8dc4f492db55eb.png)" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198212 1069 "e-13321" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198212 1071 "Let $I = [0,1] \\subset \\mathbb{R}$ and $X_i = [-i, 0] \\times [-1,1]$. Sketch a representation of $\\displaystyle \\bigcap_{i \\in I} X_i$ in the Cartesian plane." 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198212 1072 #uuid "67569060-2f33-5a8d-ae73-690742d3d318" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198212 1074 "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n\\bigcap_{i \\in I} A_i = \\big\\{x \\:: \\: x \\in A_k \\:\\:\\textrm{for all}\\:\\: k \\in I \\big\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=0.5$, we have\n\n$$\nX_{0.5} = [-0.5,0] \\times [-1, 1] = \\big\\{(a,b) \\:: \\: a \\in [-0.5, 0], b \\in [-1, 1] \\big\\}\n$$\n\nThis represents a rectangle whose vertices are at $(-0.5, -1)$, $(-0.5, 1)$, $(0, 1)$, and $(0, -1)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/8a/8ad650cb3f2fa4da920a442e8cc1b24af5027595cba4e43138eff2126cf4c102.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\nX_i = [-i, 0] \\times [-1,1] = \\big\\{(a,b) \\:: \\: a \\in [-i, 0], b \\in [-1, 1] \\big\\}\n$$\n\nis the rectangle whose vertices are at $(-i,-1)$, $(-i,1)$, $(0, 1)$, and $(0, -1)$.\n\n![](images/a3/a37cfa508cf3a60536084fbc1e57c5aeac3414904d2ad72ef1deda85dc4eddd8.png)\n\nThe intersection of all these rectangles is the line segment $\\{0\\}\\times [-1,1]{:}$\n\n![](images/5e/5e9a611629f4370c57e77a057439c759670afa17c14c4b2339e1d471f616ea72.png)" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198213 1069 "e-13322" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198213 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. If $5 \\mid m$ then $25 \\mid m$\n2. If $25 \\mid m$ then $10 \\mid m$\n3. If $25 \\mid m$ then $5 \\mid m$" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198213 1072 #uuid "e16c9c14-7461-51b3-b16a-e62e76527719" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198213 1074 "For any integers $a$, $b$, and $c$, we have transitivity of divisibility:\n\n> $a \\mid b \\quad$ and $\\quad b \\mid c$ $\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad$ $a \\mid c$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. As a counterexample, let ${\\color{blue}{m}}={\\color{blue}{5}}$. Then, $5\\mid {\\color{blue}{5}}$ but $25\\not \\mid {\\color{blue}{5}}$.\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, let ${\\color{blue}{m}}={\\color{blue}{25}}$. Then, $25\\mid {\\color{blue}{25}}$ but $10\\not \\mid {\\color{blue}{25}}$.\n- Statement III is true. First, note that $5 \\mid 25$. Therefore, we have\n$5\\mid 25$ and $25\\mid m$, which implies that $5 \\mid m$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198214 1069 "e-13324" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198214 1071 "Let $N = 10^{n}a_{n} + \\cdots + 10^{2}a_{2} + 10a_{1} + a_0$ be a positive integer,\nwhere $a_{n}, \\ldots, a_2, a_{1}, a_{0}$ are the digits of the number. Prove that if $13 \\mid N$, then\n$13 \\mid \\big(10^{n-1}a_{n} + \\cdots + 10a_{2} + a_{1} + 4a_0 \\big)$." 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198214 1072 #uuid "4a21148b-be3d-51d9-bae1-743960130617" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198214 1074 "We need to prove the implication\n\n$$\n13 \\mid N \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad 13 \\mid \\big(10^{n-1}a_{n} + \\cdots + 10a_{2} + a_{1} + 4a_0 \\big)\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the statement in terms of congruences:\n\n> According to the definition of modular congruence,\n> $13 \\mid N \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad N \\equiv 0 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\:13)$.\n\nNow, let's expand the left-hand side:\n\n> Substituting the expression for $N$ into the congruence, we get\n> $10^{n}a_{n} + ⋯ + 10^{2}a_{2} + 10a_{1} + a_{0} \\mid ≡0 \\mid (mod13)$.\n\nNow, we need to show that this implies\n\n$$\n10^{n - 1}a_{n} + ⋯ + 10a_{2} + a_{1} + 4a_{0}≡0 \\mid (mod13)\n$$\n\nThe idea is as follows:\n\n- We add $13ka_0$ for some integer $k$ to the left-hand side. This will give an expression that's also congruent to zero.\n- The value of $k$ that we pick should give a multiple of $10a_0$ when combined with the existing $a_0$ term. This allows us to factor $10$ from the left-hand side.\n\nWith this in mind, note that\n\n$$\na_0+3\\cdot 13 a_0 = a_0+39 a_0 = 40a_0\n$$\n\nSo, we proceed as follows:\n\n> Now, without changing the congruence, we can add $39a_0$ to the left-hand side since it's congruent to $0$ modulo $13{:}$\n> $\\begin{bmatrix}10^{n}a_{n} + ⋯ + 10^{2}a_{2} + 10a_{1} + a_{0} + 39a_{0} & ≡0 & (mod13) \\\\ 10^{n}a_{n} + ⋯ + 10^{2}a_{2} + 10a_{1} + 40a_{0} & ≡0 & (mod13) \\\\ 10(10^{n - 1}a_{n} + ⋯ + 10a_{2} + a_{1} + 4a_{0}) & ≡0 & (mod13)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNext, we cancel out the factor of $10$.\n\n> Since $10$ and $13$ are coprime, we can use a division rule for congruences and cancel out the factor of $10$ to get\n> $10^{n - 1}a_{n} + ⋯ + 10a_{2} + a_{1} + 4a_{0} \\mid ≡0 \\mid (mod13)$.\n\nFinally, we can write down that conclusion.\n\n> Therefore, according to the definition of modular congruence, the final equation means that\n> $13 \\mid \\big(10^{n-1}a_{n} + \\cdots + 10a_{2} + a_{1} + 4a_0 \\big)$.\n\n**Note:** All the transformations we performed above also work in reverse, which means that the right-to-left implication is also true.\n\nThe obtained equivalence statement is known in arithmetic as the rule of divisibility by $13{:}$\n\n> *\"A number is divisible by $13$ if and only if the sum of four times the last digit and the number formed by the rest of the digits is divisible by $13$.\"*" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198215 1069 "e-13325" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198215 1071 "Prove that $4^{2n-3}+ 5$ is divisible by $3$ for any integer $n \\geq 2$." 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198215 1072 #uuid "eb9557a5-b525-5911-aab8-4a345b138dd2" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198215 1074 "In this case, we need to show the following:\n\n$$\nn \\geq 2 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad 3 \\mid (4^{2n-3}+ 5)\n$$\n\nWe start by checking that the statement makes sense in the given domain of $n{:}$\n\n> Notice that the expression $4^{2n-3}+ 5$ gives us an integer value when $n \\geq 2$.\n\nRecall that $n \\mid a$ is equivalent to $a \\equiv 0 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:n)$. So, instead of proving divisibility by $3$, we can show that our expression is congruent to $0$ modulo $3{:}$\n\n> According to the definition of modular congruence,\n> $3 \\mid (4^{2n-3}+ 5) \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad 4^{2n-3}+ 5 \\equiv 0 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:3)$.\n\nNow, we proceed as follows:\n\n> Working in modulo $3$, we have the following:\n> $4^{2n - 3} + 5 \\mid ≡(1)^{2n - 3} + 2\\begin{vmatrix}(mod3) \\\\ ≡1 + 2\\end{vmatrix}(mod3); ≡3\\begin{vmatrix}(mod3) \\\\ ≡0\\end{vmatrix}(mod3)$\n\nFinally, we make our conclusion:\n\n> Therefore, $3 \\mid (4^{2n-3}+ 5)$, as required." 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198216 1069 "e-13326" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198216 1071 "Prove that $n^3-n$ is divisible by $3{:}$" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198216 1072 #uuid "e6047f6e-5482-562b-bd6d-d6d8618ffd0d" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198216 1074 "Recall that $n \\mid a$ is equivalent to $a \\equiv 0 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:n)$. So, instead of proving divisibility by $3$, we can show that our expression is congruent to $0$ modulo $3{:}$\n\n> According to the definition of modular congruence,\n> $3 \\mid (n^3-n) \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad n^3-n \\equiv 0 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:3)$.\n\nAn integer can have only $0$, $1$, or $2$ as a remainder when divided by $3$. So, we'll prove our congruence using cases.\n\n> Now, we have the following cases for $n{:}$\n\nFirst, we take $n \\equiv 0$ in our expression:\n\n> **Case 1.** Suppose $n \\equiv 0 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:3)$. Then:\n> $n^{3} - n \\mid ≡(0)^{3} - (0)\\begin{vmatrix}(mod3) \\\\ ≡0\\end{vmatrix}(mod3)$\n\nNext, we take $n \\equiv 1{:}$\n\n> **Case 2.** Suppose $n \\equiv 1 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:3)$. Then:\n> $n^{3} - n \\mid ≡(1)^{3} - (1)\\begin{vmatrix}(mod3) \\\\ ≡0\\end{vmatrix}(mod3)$\n\nThen, we take $n \\equiv 2{:}$\n\n> **Case 3.** Suppose $n \\equiv 2 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:3)$. Then:\n> $n^{3} - n \\mid ≡(2)^{3} - (2)\\begin{vmatrix}(mod3) \\\\ ≡6\\end{vmatrix}(mod3); ≡0 \\mid (mod3)$\n\nFinally, we make our conclusion:\n\n> In all these cases, we obtain\n> $n^3-n \\equiv 0 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\:3)$.\n> Therefore, $n^3-n$ is divisible by $3$, as required." 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198217 1069 "e-13329" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198217 1071 "Suppose we wish to construct a proof by contrapositive of the following statement:\n\n> Let $m$ and $n$ be integers. If $m +3n$ is odd, then $m$ is even or $n$ is even.\n\nA proof schema is outlined below. Fill in the missing entries.\n\nThe contrapositive form of the given statement is as follows:\n\n$m \\, \\, \\bbox[3pt,border:solid black 1pt]{\\phantom{even}} {\\phantom{}} \\, \\, \\, \\text{and} \\, \\, \\, n \\, \\, \\bbox[3pt,border:solid black 1pt]{\\phantom{even}} {\\phantom{}} \\, \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad m + 3n \\, \\, \\bbox[3pt,border:solid black 1pt]{\\phantom{for any}} {\\phantom{}} \\,$\n\nIf $m$ and $n$ are $\\bbox[3pt,border:solid black 1pt]{\\phantom{even}}$, then\n$m = \\bbox[3pt,border:solid black 1pt]{\\phantom{even}} \\quad\\text{and}\\quad n = \\bbox[3pt,border:solid black 1pt]{\\phantom{even}}$,\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are $\\bbox[3pt,border:solid black 1pt]{\\phantom{integers}}$.\n\nSubstituting $m = \\bbox[3pt,border:solid black 1pt]{\\phantom{even}} \\, \\, \\, \\text{and}\\, \\, \\, n = \\bbox[3pt,border:solid black 1pt]{\\phantom{even}}$ into the expression $m +3n$, we can show that $m +3n= \\bbox[3pt,border:solid black 1pt]{\\phantom{even}}$, where $c$ is $\\bbox[3pt,border:solid black 1pt]{\\phantom{even}}$\n\nTherefore, since $m +3n$ $\\bbox[3pt,border:solid black 1pt]{\\phantom{is one more}}$ a multiple of $2$, we conclude that $m +3n$ is $\\bbox[3pt,border:solid black 1pt]{\\phantom{even}}$." 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198217 1072 #uuid "8d0ba17c-dcd0-5437-aaf3-3db668b00c62" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198217 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p$.\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1$.\n\nIn this case, we need to prove the following:\n\n$$\nm +3n \\text{is odd} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad m \\text{is even or} n \\text{is even}\n$$\n\nTo prove $P \\Rightarrow Q$, it suffices to directly prove the contrapositive\n\n$$\n\\neg Q \\Rightarrow \\neg P\n$$\n\n$$\n\\neg \\left(m \\text{is even or} n \\text{is even}\\right) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\neg \\left(m +3n \\text{is odd}\\right)\n$$\n\nNegating the right-hand-side of our implication is straightforward:\n\n$$\n\\neg \\left(m \\text{is even or} n \\text{is even}\\right) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad m +3n \\text{is even}\n$$\n\nTo negate the statement \"$m$ is even or $n$ is even\", we use De Morgan's law for disjunctions.\n\n$$\n¬(mis even ornis even) \\mid ⇔¬(mis even∨nis even); ⇔¬(mis even)∧¬(nis even); ⇔mis odd∧nis odd; ⇔mis odd andnis odd\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following statement:\n\n> The contrapositive form of the given statement is as follows:\n> $\\color{blue} m\\,\\, \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\text{is odd}} \\text{and} n\\,\\, \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\text{is odd}} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad m +3n\\,\\, \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}{\\text{is even}}}$.\n\nWe start by assuming $m$ and $n$ are odd.\n\n> If $m$ and $n$ are $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{odd}}$, then\n> $m = \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2a+1} \\quad\\text{and}\\quad n = \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2b+1}$\n> where $a$ and $b$ are $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{integers}}$.\n\nThe idea is to substitute\n\n$$\nm=2a+1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn=2b+1\n$$\n\ninto the expression $m +3n$ and show it is even by writing it as a multiple of $2$.\n\n> Substituting $m=$ $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2a+1}$ and $n=$ $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2b+1}$ into the expression $m +3n$, we can show that\n> $m +3n = \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2c}$,\n> where $c$ is $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{an integer}}$.\n\nOnce we've done this, the proof is complete, and we can state our conclusion.\n\n> Therefore, since $m +3n$ $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{is}}$ a multiple of $2$, we conclude that $m +3n$ is $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{even}}$." 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198218 1069 "e-13330" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198218 1071 "Construct a proof schema to prove the following statement:\n\n> Let $m$ and $n$ be integers. Then, $m+3n$ is even only if $m$ and $n$ have the same parity." 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198218 1072 #uuid "d9407934-d4bb-5727-af80-21528732ec42" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198218 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p$.\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1$.\n\nIn this case, we need to prove the following:\n\n$$\nm+3n \\text{is even} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad m \\text{and} n \\text{have the same parity}\n$$\n\nTo prove $P \\Rightarrow Q$, it suffices to directly prove the contrapositive\n\n$$\n\\neg Q \\Rightarrow \\neg P\n$$\n\n> The contrapositive form of the given statement is as follows:\n> $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue} m \\text{and} n \\text{have opposite parity}} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue} m+3n \\text{is odd}}$.\n\nNow, we have two cases: $m$ is even and $n$ is odd, or $m$ is odd and $n$ is even.\n\n- First, we consider the case when $m$ is even.\n\n> **Case 1**. If $m$ is even, then $n$ is $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{odd}}$ and we have\n> $m = {\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2a}} \\quad\\text{and}\\quad n = {\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2b+1}}$,\n> where $a$\n> Substituting $m = 2a$ and $n = 2b + 1$ into the expression $m+3n$, we can show that $m+3n = \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2c+1}$, where $c$ is $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{an integer}}$.\n> Hence, since $m+3n$ $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{is one larger than}}$ a multiple of $2$, we conclude that $m+3n$ is $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{odd}}$.\n\n- Now, we consider the case when $m$ is odd.\n\n> **Case 2**. If $m$ is odd, then $n$ is $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{even}}$ and we have\n> $m = {\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2a+1}} \\quad\\text{and}\\quad n = {\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2b}}$,\n> where $a$\n> Substituting $m = 2a + 1$ and $n = 2b$ into the expression $m+3n$, we can show that $m+3n = \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2c+1}$, where $c$ is $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{an integer}}$.\n> Hence, since $m+n$ $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{is one larger than}}$ a multiple of $2$, we conclude that $m+3n$ is $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{odd}}$.\n\nOnce we've done this, the proof is complete, and we can state our conclusion.\n\n> Finally, combining the cases above, we conclude that $m+3n$ is $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{even}}$ only if $m$ and $n$ have $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{the same}}$ parity." 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198219 1069 "e-13331" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198219 1071 "Let $m$ and $n$ be integers. Prove that $(m-1)n^2$ is odd only if $m$ is even and $n$ is odd." 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198219 1072 #uuid "384b71db-aa13-58e7-9f51-cf5e5c78d9f7" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198219 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p$.\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1$.\n\nIn this case, we need to prove the following:\n\n$$\n(m-1)n^2 \\text{is odd} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad m \\text{is even and} n \\text{is odd}\n$$\n\nTo prove $P \\Rightarrow Q$, it suffices to prove the contrapositive\n\n$$\n\\neg Q \\Rightarrow \\neg P\n$$\n\n$$\n\\neg\\left(m \\textrm{is even and} n \\textrm{is odd} \\right) \\Rightarrow \\neg\\left((m-1)n^2\\textrm{is odd}\\right)\n$$\n\nNegating the right-hand-side of our implication is straightforward:\n\n$$\n\\neg\\left(m \\textrm{is even and} n \\textrm{is odd} \\right) \\Rightarrow (m-1)n^2 \\textrm{is even}\n$$\n\nTo negate the statement \"$m$ is even and $n$ is odd\", we use De Morgan's law for conjunctions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n¬(mis even andnis odd) &= ¬(mis even∧nis odd) \\\\\n&= ¬(mis even)∨¬(nis odd) \\\\\n&= mis odd∨nis even \\\\\n&= mis odd ornis even\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's write down the contrapositive of our statement.\n\n> The contrapositive form of the given statement is as follows:\n> $m \\text{is odd or} n \\text{is even} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad (m-1)n^2 \\text{is even}$\n> We'll prove this statement instead of the original one.\n\nNow, we have two cases: $m$ is odd, or $n$ is even.\n\n- First, we consider the case when $m$ is odd.\n\n> **Case 1**. If $m$ is odd, then $m=2a+1$ for some integer $a$.\n> As a result, we obtain\n> $(m - 1)n^{2}|= (2a + 1 - 1)n^{2}; = 2an^{2}; = 2(an^{2})_(⏟)_(b)$.\n> Let $b=an^2$. Since $a$ and $n$ are integers, we have that $b$ is an integer.\n> Therefore, we have\n> $(m - 1)n^{2}|= 2(an^{2})_(⏟)_(b); = 2b$,\n> which is a multiple of $2$. Hence, $(m-1)n^2$ is even.\n\n- Now, we consider the case when $n$ is even.\n\n> **Case 2**. If $n$ is even, then $n=2a$ for some integer $a$.\n> As a result, we obtain\n> $(m - 1)n^{2}|= (m - 1)(2a)^{2}; = 4(m - 1)a^{2}; = 2(2(m - 1)a^{2})_(⏟)_(b)$.\n> Let $b = 2(m-1)a^2$. Since $a$ and $m$ are integers, we have that $b$ is an integer.\n> Therefore, we have\n> $(m - 1)n^{2}|= 2(2(m - 1)a^{2})_(⏟)_(b); = 2b$,\n> which is a multiple of $2$. Hence, $(m-1)n^2$ is even.\n\nFinally, we write our conclusion.\n\n> Therefore, combining the cases above, we conclude that $(m-1)n^2$ is odd only if $m$ is even and $n$ is odd." 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198220 1069 "e-13676" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198220 1071 "Estimate the difference $7.6 - 2.18$ using benchmarks with decimal parts of $0.0$ or $0.5$." 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198220 1072 #uuid "4cc3d87a-4c21-58d8-bae8-4f0f89b15777" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198220 1074 "First, we round both numbers to the nearest benchmark:\n$7.6\\begin{vmatrix}- & 2.18 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 7.5 & -\\end{vmatrix}2.0$\n\nNext, we calculate the difference:\n$7\\begin{vmatrix}. & 5 \\\\ - & 2\\end{vmatrix}.\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{vmatrix}. \\mid 5$\n\nTherefore, $7.6 - 2.18$ is approximately $5.5$." 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198221 1069 "e-13678" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198221 1071 "Three years ago, Rick was $4.43$ feet tall. He's now $5.11$ feet tall. Using benchmarks with decimal parts of $0.0$, or $0.5$, estimate how much Rick grew during this period." 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198221 1072 #uuid "eaa99845-f4fd-56ba-bdbb-e9cb80bfd465" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198221 1074 "To estimate how much Rick has grown during this period, we have to estimate the difference $5.11-4.43$.\n\nFirst, we round both numbers to the nearest benchmark:\n$5.11\\begin{vmatrix}- & 4.43 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 5.0 & -\\end{vmatrix}4.5$\n\nNext, we calculate the difference:\n\n$$\n4\\begin{vmatrix}10 \\\\ 5\\end{vmatrix}.\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\\\ -\\end{vmatrix}4\\begin{vmatrix}. & 5 \\\\ 0 &.\\end{vmatrix}5\n$$\n\nTherefore, Rick grew by approximately $0.5$ feet during this period." 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198222 1069 "e-13998" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198222 1071 "Consider the following system of equations.\n${3a + 2b =-2; 4a - 3b = 3$\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $a$ from the system:\n\nTo eliminate $a$, multiply the first equation by $44$\nthe second by $44$ and add the equations." 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198222 1072 #uuid "b618dc4c-996c-501c-972c-1bcfdeee32be" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198222 1074 "Notice that the coefficient of $a$ in the first equation is ${\\color{red}{3}}$, while in the second equation it is ${\\color{blue}{4}}$.\n\nIf we multiply the first equation by ${\\color{blue}{4}}$ and the second equation by $-{\\color{red}{3}}$, then the $a$-coefficient in the first and second equations will become $12$ and $-12$, and these will cancel when we add the equations.\n\n$$\n{(3a + 2b =-2) \\cdot (4); (4a - 3b = 3) \\cdot (-3)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $a$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12a + 8b &= -8 \\\\\n- 12a + 9b &= -9 \\\\\n(8b + 9b) &= (-8 - 9) \\\\\n17b &= -17\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n> *To eliminate $a$, multiply the first equation by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}4}$, the second by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-3}$, and add the equations.*" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198223 1069 "e-14001" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198223 1071 "Consider the following system of equations.\n${4p + 5q = 7; 3p - 2q =-12$\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $q$ from the system:\n\nTo eliminate $q$, multiply the first equation by $44$\nthe second by $44$ and add the equations." 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198223 1072 #uuid "68da240e-2c79-50d7-86dd-d203d01dcb32" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198223 1074 "Notice that the coefficient of $q$ in the first equation is ${\\color{red}{5}}$, while in the second equation it is $-{\\color{blue}{2}}$.\n\nIf we multiply the first equation by ${\\color{blue}{2}}$ and the second equation by ${\\color{red}{5}}$, then the $q$-coefficient in the first and second equations will become $10$ and $-10$, and these will cancel when we add the equations.\n\n$$\n{(4p + 5q = 7) \\cdot (2); (3p - 2q =-12) \\cdot (5)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $q$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8p + 10q &= 14 \\\\\n15p - 10q &= -60 \\\\\n(8p + 15p) &= (14 - 60) \\\\\n23p &= -46\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n> *To eliminate $q$, multiply the first equation by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2}$, the second by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}5}$, and add the equations.*" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198224 1069 "e-14003" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198224 1071 "Find the solution $(x,y)$ to the following system of equations by eliminating the variable $x{:}$\n\n${2x - 3y =-7; - 3x + 4y = 8$" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198224 1072 #uuid "c04ac5ff-446a-52d8-b464-88d38efd6088" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198224 1074 "Notice that the $x$-coefficient in the first equation is ${\\color{red}2}$, while in the second equation it is $-{\\color{blue}3}$.\n\nIf we multiply the first equation by ${\\color{blue}3}$ and the second equation by ${\\color{red}2}$, then the $x$-coefficient in the first and second equations will become $6$ and $-6$, and these will cancel when we add the equations.\n\n$$\n{(2x - 3y =-7) \\cdot (3); (-3x + 4y = 8) \\cdot (2)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $x$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6x - 9y &= -21 \\\\\n- 6x + 8y &= 16 \\\\\n(8y - 9y) &= (16 - 21) \\\\\n- y &= -5 \\\\\ny &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $y=5$. However, we still need to find $x$. To do this, we substitute $y=5$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x - 3y &= -7 \\\\\n2x - 3(5) &= -7 \\\\\n2x - 15 &= -7 \\\\\n2x &= 8 \\\\\nx &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=4$ and $y=5$, which can be written as $(4,5)$." 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198225 1069 "e-14004" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198225 1071 "Find the solution $(x,y)$ to the system below by eliminating the variable $y$.\n\n${4x + 3y = 3; 5x + 4y = 5$" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198225 1072 #uuid "10d6a803-5114-5995-87c3-1b0e0e87a079" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198225 1074 "Notice that the coefficient of $y$ in the first equation is ${\\color{red}3}$, while in the second equation it is ${\\color{blue}4}$.\n\nIf we multiply the first equation by ${\\color{blue}4}$ and the second equation by $-{\\color{red}3}$, then the $y$-coefficient in the first and second equations will become $-12$ and $12$, and these will cancel when we add the equations.\n\n$$\n{(4x + 3y = 3) \\cdot (4); (5x + 4y = 5) \\cdot (-3)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n16x + 12y &= 12 \\\\\n- 15x - 12y &= -15 \\\\\n(16x - 15x) &= (12 - 15) \\\\\nx &= -3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x=-3$. However, we still need to find $y$. To do this, we substitute $x=-3$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x + 3y &= 3 \\\\\n4(-3) + 3y &= 3 \\\\\n- 12 + 3y &= 3 \\\\\n3y &= 15 \\\\\ny &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-3$ and $y=5$, which can be written as $(-3,5)$." 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198226 1069 "e-14017" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198226 1071 "Determine whether the following system of equations is consistent or inconsistent. If it is consistent, indicate whether it is consistent dependent or consistent independent.\n${x - 2y - 2 = 0; 4x - 8y = 8$" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198226 1072 #uuid "81cc631d-2430-5db0-aa17-718a6f090c42" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198226 1074 "A system of equations is *consistent* if it has at least one solution. Otherwise, it is called *inconsistent*.\n\nConsistent systems, in turn, can be of the following two types:\n\n- When a system of equations has a *single* solution, it is called *consistent independent*.\n- When a system of equations has an *infinite number* of solutions, it is called *consistent dependent*.\n\nWe can summarize these definitions using the diagram shown below.\n\n![](images/16/1692fbea298339ff839cf2f14986d51af45d1ec72b2b7d0de179336bdef40c0e.png)\n\nWe will solve the system using substitution.\n\nFirst, we can solve the first equation for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - 2y - 2 &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 2 + 2y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can substitute\n\n$$\nx=2+2y\n$$\n\ninto the second equation to obtain an equation that we can solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x - 8y &= 8 \\\\\n4(2 + 2y) - 8y &= 8 \\\\\n8 + 8y - 8y &= 8 \\\\\n8 &= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause we obtained a true statement, we conclude that there are infinitely many solutions to the system. Therefore, the system is consistent dependent." 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198227 1069 "e-14553" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198227 1071 "Estimate the difference $9.17 - 5.4$ using benchmarks with decimal parts of $0.0$ or $0.5$." 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198227 1072 #uuid "031d1958-8438-555e-8a1c-6c5a2c5a8066" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198227 1074 "First, we round both numbers to the nearest benchmark:\n$9.17\\begin{vmatrix}- & 5.4 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 9.0 & -\\end{vmatrix}5.5$\n\nNext, we calculate the difference:\n$8\\begin{vmatrix}10 \\\\ 9\\end{vmatrix}.\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\\\ -\\end{vmatrix}5\\begin{vmatrix}. & 5 \\\\ 3 &.\\end{vmatrix}5$\n\nTherefore, $9.17 - 5.4$ is approximately $3.5$." 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198228 1069 "e-14985" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198228 1071 "Given that $A$, $B$, and $C$ are sets, $(A \\cap B) \\cup (A \\cap C) \\subseteq A \\cap (B \\cup C)$.\n\nA proof of this statement is given below.\n\n> $\\textrm{L1}$: Let $x \\in (A \\cap B) \\cup (A \\cap C)$.\n> $\\textrm{L2}$: Then, $x \\in A \\cap B$ or $x \\in A \\cap C$.\n> $\\textrm{L3}$: **Case 1:** $x \\in A \\cap B$\n> $\\textrm{L4}$: $\\Rightarrow x \\in A \\,\\text{and} \\, x \\in B$\n> $\\textrm{L5}$: $\\Rightarrow x \\in A \\,\\text{and} x \\in B \\cup C$\n> $\\textrm{L6}$: $\\Rightarrow A \\cap (B \\cup C)$.\n> $\\textrm{L7}$: **Case 2:** Similarly, if $x \\in A \\cap C$, then $x \\in A \\,\\text{and} x \\in B \\cup C \\Rightarrow x\\in A \\cap (B \\cup C)$.\n> $\\textrm{L8}$: In both cases, $x \\in A \\cap (B \\cup C)$.\n> $\\textrm{L9}$: Therefore, $(A \\cap B) \\cup (A \\cap C) \\subseteq A \\cap (B \\cup C)$.\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n- Line $\\textrm{L2}$ follows from line $\\textrm{L1}$ by the definition of $\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_$.\n- Line $\\textrm{L4}$ follows from line $\\textrm{L3}$ by the definition of $\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_$.\n- Line $\\textrm{L5}$ follows from line $\\textrm{L4}$ due to the fact that $\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_$ for any sets $X$ and $Y$." 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198228 1072 #uuid "628a4323-ced8-5c73-824c-8ab32d6f8200" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198228 1074 "Let's examine each statement in turn. Assume $X$ and $Y$ are sets.\n\n- First, we consider lines $\\textrm{L1}$ and $\\textrm{L2}$. We have the following equivalence:\n$a\\in (X \\cup Y) \\quad \\Leftrightarrow \\quad (a \\in X) \\textrm{or} (a \\in Y)$\nTherefore, line $\\textrm{L2}$ follows from line $\\textrm{L1}$ by the definition of $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{the union of sets}}$.\n- Next, we consider lines $\\textrm{L3}$ and $\\textrm{L4}$. We have the following equivalence:\n$a\\in (X \\cap Y) \\quad \\Leftrightarrow \\quad (a\\in X) \\textrm{and} (a \\in Y)$\nTherefore, line $\\textrm{L4}$ follows from line $\\textrm{L3}$ by the definition of $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{the intersection of sets}}$.\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\textrm{L4}$ and $\\textrm{L5}$. Since\n$B \\subseteq B \\cup C$,\nand $x \\in B$, it follows that $x\\in B \\cup C$. Therefore, $\\textrm{L5}$ follows from line $\\textrm{L4}$ due to the fact that $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}X\\subseteq X\\cup Y}$ for any sets $X$ and $Y$." 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198229 1069 "e-14988" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198229 1071 "Consider the following statement:\n\n> Given that $A$ and $B$ are sets, $\\overline{A \\cap B} = \\overline{A} \\cup \\overline{B}$.\n\nA part of the proof showing that $\\overline{A \\cap B} \\subseteq \\overline{A} \\cup \\overline{B}$ is given below.\n\n> $\\textrm{L1}{:}\\:$ $x \\in \\overline{A \\cap B}$\n> $\\textrm{L2}{:}\\:$ $\\Rightarrow x \\notin A \\cap B$\n> $\\textrm{L3}{:}\\:$ $\\Rightarrow \\lnot (x \\in A \\cap B)$\n> $\\textrm{L4}{:}\\:$ $\\Rightarrow \\lnot ((x \\in A) \\land (x \\in B))$\n> $\\textrm{L5}{:}\\:$ $\\Rightarrow \\lnot (x \\in A) \\lor \\lnot (x \\in B)$\n> $\\textrm{L6}{:}\\:$ $\\Rightarrow (x \\notin A) \\lor (x \\notin B)$\n> $\\textrm{L7}{:}\\:$ $\\Rightarrow (x \\in \\overline{A}) \\lor (x \\in \\overline{B})$\n> $\\textrm{L8}{:}\\:$ $\\Rightarrow x \\in \\overline{A} \\cup \\overline{B}$\n\nComplete the blanks in the following reasoning.\n\n- Line $\\textrm{L5}$ follows from line $\\textrm{L4}$ by\n$\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_$.\n- Line $\\textrm{L7}$ follows from line $\\textrm{L6}$ by the definition of\n$\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_$.\n\n- Line $\\textrm{L8}$ follows from line $\\textrm{L7}$ by the definition of\n$\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_$.\n- To complete the given proof, we must also show that if $x \\in$\n$\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_$, then $x\\in\\, \\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_$." 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198229 1072 #uuid "64e650c7-eae2-5699-891d-eb0f5b9f0bf6" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198229 1074 "Let's examine each statement in turn. Assume $X$ and $Y$ are sets, and $P$ and $Q$ are statements.\n\n- First, we consider lines $\\textrm{L4}$ and $\\textrm{L5}$. De Morgan's law for conjunctions states the following:\n$\\lnot (P \\land Q) \\equiv \\lnot P \\lor \\lnot Q$\nTherefore, line $\\textrm{L5}$ follows from line $\\textrm{L4}$ by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{De Morgan's law}}$ $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{for conjunctions}}$.\n- Next, we consider lines $\\textrm{L6}$ and $\\textrm{L7}$. According to the definition of the set complement, we have the following equivalence:\n$x \\notin X \\quad \\Leftrightarrow \\quad x \\in \\overline{X}$\nTherefore, line $\\textrm{L7}$ follows from line $\\textrm{L6}$ by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{the definition of}}$ $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{the set complement}}$.\n- Then, we consider lines $\\textrm{L7}$ and $\\textrm{L8}$. According to the definition of the union of sets, we have the following equivalence:\n$a \\in X \\cup Y \\quad \\Leftrightarrow \\quad (a \\in X) \\lor (a \\in Y)$\nTherefore, line $\\textrm{L8}$ follows from line $\\textrm{L7}$ by the definition of $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{the union of sets}}$.\n\n- Finally, to prove that two sets are equal, we must show that a fixed but arbitrary element of one set is an element of another, and vice versa. Therefore, to complete the given proof, we must also demonstrate that if $x \\in \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\overline{A} \\cup \\overline{B}}$ then $x \\in \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\overline{A \\cap B}}$." 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198230 1069 "e-15010" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198230 1071 "Consider a system of two linear equations with two variables, where each equation is represented by the corresponding straight line in the plane. What can we say about the intersections of these lines, given that the system is\n\n1. inconsistent,\n2. consistent independent, or\n3. consistent dependent?" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198230 1072 #uuid "98b7e453-0b79-5710-9c47-1b6fd7fad6a3" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198230 1074 "The solutions of a linear system with two variables correspond to the points of intersection of the two lines defined by the equations. Three possibilities exist, as shown below.\n\n![](images/8c/8c544df38ce82dd70c3d01718233c9fae1fae0534748892643a12b6556918fb7.png)\n\nIn other words, we have the following cases:\n\n- If a system is *inconsistent* (has no solutions), the corresponding lines are parallel (do not intersect). And vice versa, parallel lines correspond to an inconsistent system.\n- If a system is *consistent independent* (i.e., has exactly one solution), the corresponding lines intersect at exactly one point. And vice versa, intersecting lines correspond to a consistent independent system.\n- If a system is *consistent dependent* (i.e., has infinitely many solutions), the corresponding lines intersect at infinitely many points (coincide). And vice versa, coinciding lines correspond to a consistent dependent system." 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198231 1069 "e-15011" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198231 1071 "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n${y = 2x + 6; y = 2x + 1$\n\nComment on the consistency of this system, the corresponding number of solutions, and its geometric interpretation." 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198231 1072 #uuid "b68f54e2-49f7-5c15-87eb-776fbd84d8f0" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198231 1074 "A system of equations is *consistent* if it has at least one solution. Otherwise, it is called *inconsistent*.\n\nConsistent systems, in turn, can be of the following two types:\n\n- When a system of equations has a *single* solution, it is called *consistent independent*.\n- When a system of equations has an *infinite number* of solutions, it is called *consistent dependent*.\n\nWe can summarize these definitions using the diagram shown below.\n\n![](images/16/1692fbea298339ff839cf2f14986d51af45d1ec72b2b7d0de179336bdef40c0e.png)\n\nThe solutions of a linear system with two variables correspond to the points of intersection of the two lines defined by the equations. Three possibilities exist, as shown below.\n\n![](images/8c/8c544df38ce82dd70c3d01718233c9fae1fae0534748892643a12b6556918fb7.png)\n\nNotice that the equations in the system have the same slopes ($2$) but distinct $y$-intercepts ($6$ and $1$, respectively). So, the lines $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{are parallel}}$ and do not intersect.\n\nTherefore, the system has $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{no solutions}}$, meaning that it's $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{inconsistent}}$." 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198232 1069 "e-15024" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198232 1071 "Given that\n$A \\cap C = \\{1,2,4\\}, \\quad B\\cap D = \\{0,3\\}$,\nwhat is $(A \\times B) \\cap (C \\times D)$?" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198232 1072 #uuid "7afc8769-6f9f-54c1-93d7-60da93e1f083" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198232 1074 "We have the following identity for all sets $A,B,C,D{:}$\n\n$$\n(A \\times B) \\cap (C \\times D) = (A\\cap C) \\times (B\\cap D)\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nA \\cap C = \\{1,2,4\\}, \\qquad B \\cap D = \\{0,3\\}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(A \\cdot B)∩(C \\cdot D) &= (A∩C) \\cdot (B∩D). \\\\\n&= {1, 2, 4} \\cdot {0, 3} \\\\\n&= {(1, 0), (1, 3), (2, 0), (2, 3), (4, 0), (4, 3)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198233 1069 "e-2398" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198233 1071 "Find the derivative of $\\mathbf f(t) = \\langle \\sin t,\\cos t, t \\rangle$ at $t= \\dfrac{\\pi}4$." 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198233 1072 #uuid "a5637d71-22d4-548e-ad36-d09bff61d643" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198233 1074 "First, we find $\\mathbf f'(t)$ by differentiating each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(t) &= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(\\sin t), \\frac{d}{dt}(\\cos t), \\frac{d}{dt}(t)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\cos t,-\\sin t, 1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute\n\n$$\n\\mathbf f'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}4\\right)\n$$\n\nwe substitute\n\n$$\nt=\\dfrac{\\pi}4\n$$\n\ninto the expression for $\\mathbf f'(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(\\frac{π}{4}) &= ⟨\\cos (\\frac{π}{4}),-\\sin (\\frac{π}{4}), 1⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}, 1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198234 1069 "e-5264" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198234 1071 "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the ellipsoid $\\dfrac{x^2}{24} + \\dfrac{y^2}{24} +\\dfrac{z^2}{6} = 1$ and the line\n${x = 4t; y = 8t,; z = 2t$\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$." 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198234 1072 #uuid "5524aeab-cc64-5da8-bea3-8cd5dd86ebcb" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198234 1074 "Substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x^{2}}{24} + \\frac{y^{2}}{24} + \\frac{z^{2}}{6} &= 1 \\\\\nx^{2} + y^{2} + 4z^{2} &= 24 \\\\\n(4t)^{2} + (8t)^{2} + 4(2t)^{2} &= 24 \\\\\n16t^{2} + 64t^{2} + 16t^{2} &= 24 \\\\\n96t^{2} &= 24 \\\\\nt^{2} &= \\frac{1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solution\n\n$$\nt = \\pm \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-\\dfrac 1 2$, we have\n${x = 4(-\\frac{1}{2}) =-2; y = 8(-\\frac{1}{2}) =-4; z = 2(-\\frac{1}{2}) =-1$,\nso the first point is $\\left(-2,-4,-1\\right)$.\n- For $t=\\dfrac 1 2$, we have\n${x = 4(\\frac{1}{2}) = 2; y = 8(\\frac{1}{2}) = 4; z = 2(\\frac{1}{2}) = 1$,\nso the second point is $\\left(2,4,1\\right)$.\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(-2,-4,-1)$ and $(2,4,1)$." 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198235 1069 "e-5618" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198235 1071 "Find the projection of the intersection between the ellipsoid $\\dfrac{(x+1)^2}{16}+\\dfrac{y^2}{4}+\\dfrac{z^2}{16}=1$ and the plane $x-z=3$ onto the $yz$-plane." 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198235 1072 #uuid "b1f59935-cd61-5c59-aa8d-cf7ba7f151bc" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198235 1074 "Since we want to find the projection onto the $yz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $x$. First, we solve for $x$ in the equation of the plane, as follows:\n\n$$\nx - z = 3⟹x = z + 3\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $x$ into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(((z + 3) + 1)^{2})/(16) + \\frac{y^{2}}{4} + \\frac{z^{2}}{16} &= 1 \\\\\n((z + 4)^{2})/(16) + \\frac{y^{2}}{4} + \\frac{z^{2}}{16} &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{z^{2} + 8z + 16}{16} + \\frac{y^{2}}{4} + \\frac{z^{2}}{16} &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{y^{2}}{4} + \\frac{2z^{2} + 8z + 16}{16} &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{y^{2}}{4} + \\frac{z^{2} + 4z + 8}{8} &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by completing the square for the variable $z$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{y^{2}}{4} + \\frac{z^{2} + 4z + 8}{8} &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{y^{2}}{4} + ((z^{2} + 4z + 4) + 4)/(8) &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{y^{2}}{4} + ((z + 2)^{2} + 4)/(8) &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{y^{2}}{4} + ((z + 2)^{2})/(8) + \\frac{1}{2} &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{y^{2}}{4} + ((z + 2)^{2})/(8) &= \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n\\frac{y^{2}}{2} + ((z + 2)^{2})/(4) &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y^2}{2}+\\dfrac{(z+2)^2}{4} = 1\n$$" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198236 1069 "e-609" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198236 1071 "Given that $\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle 10t^4,\\, 6\\ln t\\rangle$, what is $\\mathbf r'\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)$?" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198236 1072 #uuid "c737d317-8e09-5ccb-b816-b5f56e925ba9" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198236 1074 "We differentiate each component separately.\n\n- For the $x$-component $x(t) = 10t^4$, we have\n$x'(t) = 40t^3$,\nand therefore\n$x'\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) = 40\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^3 = 5$.\n- For the $y$-component $y(t) = 6 \\ln t$, we have\n$y^{′}(t) = \\frac{6}{t}$,\nand therefore\n$y'\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) = \\dfrac{6}{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)}=12$.\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(\\frac{1}{2}) &= ⟨x^{′}(\\frac{1}{2}), y^{′}(\\frac{1}{2})⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨5, 12⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198237 1069 "e-611" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198237 1071 "Given that $\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle t^6,\\, t^5\\rangle$, Calculate $\\mathbf r''(t)$." 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198237 1072 #uuid "cde8f304-4937-5eb7-a8e1-a08355ee4d4e" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198237 1074 "To find the second derivative of a vector-valued function, we find the second derivatives of the components.\n\n- For $x(t) = t^6$, we have\n$x^{′}(t)|= 6t^{5}; x^{″}(t)|= 30t^{4}$.\n- For $y(t) = t^5$, we have\n$y^{′}(t)|= 5t^{4}; y^{″}(t)|= 20t^{3}$.\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{″}(t) &= ⟨x^{″}(t), y^{″}(t)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨30t^{4}, 20t^{3}⟩ \\\\\n&= 10⟨3t^{4}, 2t^{3}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198238 1069 "e-6878" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198238 1071 "Prove that if $m\\in\\mathbb Z$ is even and $n\\in\\mathbb Z$ is odd, then $mn$ is even." 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198238 1072 #uuid "8b458572-b433-53b1-827b-8c0a2a4a2c5a" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198238 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p$.\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1$.\n\nIn this case, we need to show the following:\n\n$$\nm \\text{is even and} n \\text{is odd} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad mn \\text{is even}\n$$\n\nWe start by using the fact that $m$ is even and $n$ is odd.\n\n> Let $m$ and $n$ be integers, where $m$ is even and $n$ is odd. Then, $m=2a$ and $n=2b+1$ for some integers $a$ and $b$.\n\n**Watch Out**! We must use two *different* integers to represent the evenness of $m$ and the oddness of $n$. In this case, we use $a$ and $b$.\n\nThe idea is to substitute $m=2a$ and\n\n$$\nn=2b+1\n$$\n\ninto our expression $mn$ and show it is even by writing it as a multiple of $2$.\n\n> Therefore, we have\n> $mn|= (2a) \\cdot (2b + 1); = 4ab + 2a; = 2(2ab + a)_(⏟)_(c)$.\n\nNow, if we define another integer\n\n$$\nc=2ab+a\n$$\n\n(highlighted above), we have that $mn = 2c$, which shows that $mn$ is indeed a multiple of $2$.\n\n> Let $c=2ab+a$. Since $a$ and $b$ are integers, $c$ is an integer.\n> Therefore,\n> $mn|= 2(2ab + a)_(⏟)_(c); = 2c$\n> which is a multiple of $2$.\n\nFinally, we write our conclusion:\n\n> Therefore, we conclude that $mn$ is even." 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198239 1069 "e-6902" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198239 1071 "Let $m$ and $n$ be integers. Prove that if $mn$ is odd, then $m$ is odd or $n$ is odd." 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198239 1072 #uuid "60ec2cc1-7b2c-5fba-8c0c-54fdecf29080" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198239 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p$.\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1$.\n\nIn this case, we need to prove the following:\n\n$$\nmn \\text{is odd} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad m \\text{is odd or} n \\text{is odd}\n$$\n\nTo prove $P \\Rightarrow Q$, it suffices to prove the contrapositive\n\n$$\n\\neg Q \\Rightarrow \\neg P\n$$\n\n$$\n\\neg\\left(m \\text{is odd or} n \\text{is odd}\\right) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\neg\\left(mn \\text{is odd}\\right)\n$$\n\nNegating the right-hand-side of our implication is straightforward:\n\n$$\n\\neg\\left(m \\text{is odd or} n \\text{is odd}\\right) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad mn\\text{is even}\n$$\n\nTo negate the statement \"$m$ is odd or $n$ is odd\", we use De Morgan's law for disjunctions.\n\n$$\n¬(mis odd ornis odd) \\mid ⇔¬(mis odd∨nis odd); ⇔¬(mis odd)∧¬(nis odd); ⇔mis even∧nis even; ⇔mis even andnis even\n$$\n\nSo, let's write down the contrapositive of our original statement.\n\n> The contrapositive form of the given statement is as follows:\n> $m \\text{is even and} n \\text{is even} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad mn \\text{is even}$\n> We'll prove this statement instead of the original one.\n\nWe start by using the fact that $m$ and $n$ are even.\n\n> If $m$ and $n$ are even, then\n> $m = 2a \\quad\\text{and}\\quad n = 2b$,\n> where $a$ and $b$ are integers.\n\nWe substitute $m=2a$ and $n=2b$ into the expression $mn$ and show it is even by writing it as a multiple of $2$.\n\n> Therefore,\n> $mn|= (2a)(2b); = 4ab; = 2(2ab)_(⏟)_(c)$.\n\nNow, if we define another integer $c=2ab$ (highlighted above), we have that $mn= 2c$, which shows that $mn$ is indeed a multiple of $2$.\n\n> Let $c=2ab$. Since $a$ and $b$ are integers, we have that $c$ is an integer.\n> Therefore, we have\n> $mn|= 2(2ab)_(⏟)_(c); = 2c$,\n> which is a multiple of $2$.\n\nFinally, we write our conclusion.\n\n> Therefore, $mn$ is even." 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198240 1069 "e-7127" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198240 1071 "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}$, where $\\mathbf T$ is defined as\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198240 1072 #uuid "d154ea1a-bb3c-510d-abe3-dc1cf51558b4" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198240 1074 "Given a transformation\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$, denoted\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(x, y))/(∂(u, v)) &= \\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $\\mathbf T$, we need to solve\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = 0\n$$\n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\nx(u,v) = u^2-v^2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny(u,v) = u+v\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} &= 2u & \\frac{∂x}{∂v}|=-2v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u}|= 1 & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}|= 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(x, y))/(∂(u, v)) &= \\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}2u & -2v \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 2(u + v)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} =0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(x, y))/(∂(u, v)) &= 0 \\\\\n2(u + v) &= 0 \\\\\nu &= -v\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the critical points of $T$ consist of those points that lie on the line $u=-v$ in the $uv$-plane." 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198241 1069 "e-7285" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198241 1071 "Consider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, given by\n$D = \\big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: -2 \\leq 4x+2y \\leq 2, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y-x \\leq 3 \\big\\}$.\nBy performing the change of variables $u = 4x+2y$ and $v=y - x$, write the following integral in terms of $u$ and $v$.\n$\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D \\sin \\left(3(x+y)\\right) \\: \\mathrm{d}x \\, \\mathrm{d}y$" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198241 1072 #uuid "9d477733-7178-5b88-926f-a457faaf344a" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198241 1074 "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\, \\mathrm d x \\, \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(x(u,v), y(u,v)) \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right| \\mathrm d u \\, \\mathrm d v\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}\n$$\n\nis the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}$.\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu = 4x + 2y, \\qquad v = y - x\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1}$, that is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $\\Delta$, which is the image of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$.\n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔ &= {(u, v): - 2 \\le u \\le 2, 0 \\le v \\le 3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$.\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(u, v))/(∂(x, y)) &= \\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T$.\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1} = \\dfrac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\neq 0\n$$\n\neverywhere inside $\\Delta$. In other words, $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta$.\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables.\n\nIn this case, we first need to solve the transformation equations\n\n$$\nu = 4x+2y, \\qquad v = y-x\n$$\n\nfor $x$ and $y$, which gives\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{u-2v}6, \\qquad y =\\dfrac{u+4v}6\n$$\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)\\sin (3(x + y))dxdy &= ∬_(Δ)\\sin (3(\\frac{u - 2v}{6} + \\frac{u + 4v}{6}))\\begin{vmatrix}(∂(x, y))/(∂(u, v)) & dudv \\\\ = ∬_(Δ)\\sin (3(\\frac{2u + 2v}{6})) & \\frac{1}{6}\\end{vmatrix}dudv \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6}\\int_{0}^{3}\\int_{-2}^{2}\\sin(u + v)dudv\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198242 1069 "e-7322" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198242 1071 "Consider the region $D$, given by\n$D = \\big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 2 \\leq \\sqrt{xy} \\leq 4, \\: 2x \\leq y \\leq 3x \\big\\}$\nand the transformation $\\mathbf T$, given by\n$\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v),y(u,v))$,\nwhere $u=\\dfrac{y}{x}$ and $v=xy$. Find the representation of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}(D)$ in the $uv$-plane." 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198242 1072 #uuid "6d2cd5bf-899f-5b81-9512-ee98489d17ac" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198242 1074 "Notice that $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}(D)$ is the image of $D$ under the action of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$.\n\nLet's find the image of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}(D)$ in the $uv$-plane. To do that, we write the region $D$ in terms of $u$ and $v$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT^{-1}(D) &= {(u, v): 2 \\le \\sqrt{xy} \\le 4, 2x \\le y \\le 3x} \\\\\n&= {(u, v): 2^{2} \\le (\\sqrt{xy})^{2} \\le 4^{2}, 2 \\le \\frac{y}{x} \\le 3} \\\\\n&= {(u, v): 4 \\le xy \\le 16, 2 \\le \\frac{y}{x} \\le 3} \\\\\n&= {(u, v): 4 \\le v \\le 16, 2 \\le u \\le 3} \\\\\n&= {(u, v): 2 \\le u \\le 3, 4 \\le v \\le 16}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since\n\n$$\n2 \\leq \\sqrt{xy}\\leq 4\n$$\n\nwe must have\n\n$$\nx \\neq 0\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nu=\\dfrac{y}{x}\n$$\n\nis well-defined." 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198243 1069 "e-7323" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198243 1071 "Let $D$ be the finite region bounded by the curve\n$(x+y)^2 + y = 3$ and the line $y=-1$.\nConsider the transformation $\\mathbf T$, given by\n$\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v),y(u,v))$,\nwhere $u=x+y$ and $v=y$. Find the representation of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}(D)$ in the $uv$-plane." 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198243 1072 #uuid "c78f087b-f0e5-58e6-a7a1-92a78c85c4d9" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198243 1074 "First, notice that we are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nx+y = u\n$$\n\nand $y = v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n(x + y)^{2} + y = 3⟹u^{2} + v = 3⟹v = 3 - u^{2}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y = v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\ny =-1⟹v =-1\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{v = 3 - u^{2}; v =-1⟹{u = ± 2; v =-1\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 2, -1)$.\n\n![](images/84/84c624aab456b9a090c9c4e6d23c82d8e1f69425050b6f3ee9d4afd18e3f0cec.png)\n\nAs a result, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}^{-1}(D) = \\big\\{(u,v) \\:: \\: -2 \\leq u \\leq 2, \\quad -1 \\leq v \\leq 3-u^2 \\big\\}\n$$" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198244 1069 "e-8182" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198244 1071 "Consider a thin plate $D$ that has the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y) = x+y$ and is enclosed between the curve $y=x$ and the $x$-axis for $0 \\leq x \\leq 1$. Given that the mass of the plate is $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ units and its center of mass is located at $(\\overline{x}, \\overline{y})$, what is the value of $\\overline{y}$?" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198244 1072 #uuid "8199670b-efe4-537b-8f8a-228d567a0944" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198244 1074 "The center of mass of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(\\overline{x},\\overline{y}) = \\left(\\dfrac{M_y}{m}, \\dfrac{M_x}{m} \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\displaystyle m = \\iint \\limits_D \\lambda (x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A$ is the mass of the lamina,\n- $\\displaystyle M_x = \\iint\\limits_D y \\, \\lambda (x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A$ is the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis, and\n- $\\displaystyle M_y = \\iint\\limits_D x \\, \\lambda (x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A$ is the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis.\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I plane region:\n\n![](images/b8/b872242423692a20a9d50658c99d9e8cee7c9b2737fb96592bc2bc35b4f72c32.png)\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\:0 \\le x \\le 1, \\: 0 \\le y \\le x \\big\\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\lambda(x,y) = x+y\n$$\n\nSince we require the $y$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM_{x} &= ∬_(D)yλ(x, y)dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)y \\cdot (x + y)dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)xy + y^{2}dA \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{x}xy + y^{2}dydx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}[\\frac{xy^{2}}{2} + \\frac{y^{3}}{3}]_{y = 0}^{y = x}dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}(\\frac{x^{3}}{2} + \\frac{x^{3}}{3})dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{6}\\int_{0}^{1}x^{3}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{6}[\\frac{1}{4}x^{4}]_{x = 0}^{x = 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{6}(\\frac{1}{4} - 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{24}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\overline{y} = \\dfrac{M_x}{m} = \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{5}{24}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)} = \\dfrac{5}{12}\n$$" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198245 1069 "e-8183" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198245 1071 "Consider the thin plate $D$ enclosed between the curve $x=\\sqrt{1-y^2}$ and the $y$-axis for $-1 \\leq y \\leq 1$. Find the $x$-coordinate of its centroid." 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198245 1072 #uuid "37d7f2ae-90b0-5e6f-83c2-7cb21ed4f089" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198245 1074 "The centroid of a lamina $D$ with area $A_D$ is given by $(x_c, y_c)$, where\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle x_c = \\dfrac{1}{A_D} \\iint\\limits_D x \\, \\: \\textrm{d}A, \\qquad \\displaystyle y_c = \\dfrac{1}{A_D} \\iint\\limits_D y \\, \\: \\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type II plane region:\n\n![](images/a7/a7992c54f85f85cab3d9f923b8daca62c410393b364d30bcfbf5cff5efc93bbd.png)\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 0 \\le x \\le \\sqrt{1-y^2}, \\: -1 \\le y \\le 1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nA_D = \\dfrac12 \\cdot \\pi\\cdot 1^2 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-coordinate of the centroid is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{c} &= \\frac{1}{A_{D}}∬_(D)xdA \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{π}\\int_{-1}^{1}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{1 - y^{2}}}xdxdy \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{π}\\int_{-1}^{1}[\\frac{x^{2}}{2}]_{x = 0}^{x = \\sqrt{1 - y^{2}}}dy \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{π}\\int_{-1}^{1}\\frac{1 - y^{2}}{2}dy \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{π}\\int_{-1}^{1}1 - y^{2}dy \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{π}[y - \\frac{y^{3}}{3}]_{-1}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{π}[(1 - \\frac{1}{3}) - (-1 + \\frac{1}{3})] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{π} \\cdot \\frac{4}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{3π}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198246 1069 "e-8690" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198246 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y) = 3x^2 - y^2$ given that $3x^2 + 2y^2\\leq 16$." 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198246 1072 #uuid "7d0da02a-d686-5bb1-ab44-00286ab01783" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198246 1074 "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving\n\n$$\n\\nabla f = \\mathbf 0\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨f_{x}(x, y), f_{y}(x, y)⟩ &= ⟨0, 0⟩ \\\\\n⟨6x,-2y⟩ &= ⟨0, 0⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has the solution\n\n$$\n(x,y)=\\left(0, 0 \\right)\n$$\n\nThis point belongs to our region.\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint\n\n$$\ng(x,y)= 3x^2 + 2y^2 = 16\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint.\n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y), \\qquad g(x,y)=16\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇f(x, y) &= λ∇g(x, y,) \\\\\n⟨f_{x}(x, y), f_{y}(x, y)⟩ &= λ⟨g_{x}(x, y), g_{y}(x, y)⟩ \\\\\n⟨6x,-2y⟩ &= λ⟨6x, 4y⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{6x = 6λx; - 2y = 4λy⇒{x(1 - λ) = 0; y(2λ + 1) = 0\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y) =16$ implies\n$2y^{2}|= 16⇒y = ± 2\\sqrt{2}$,\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(0, \\pm 2\\sqrt 2 \\right)$.\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y) =16$ implies\n$3x^{2}|= 16⇒x = ± \\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3}$,\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(\\pm \\dfrac{4\\sqrt 3}{3}, 0 \\right)$.\n- Note that assuming $\\lambda = 1$ from the first equation leads to $y=0$, which we already have a solution for, and $\\lambda = -\\dfrac12$ from the second equation leads to $x=0$, which we also have a solution for. So, we have found all the candidates on the boundary.\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n\\left(0,0 \\right), \\qquad \\left(0, \\pm 2\\sqrt 2 \\right), \\qquad \\left(\\pm \\dfrac{4\\sqrt 3}{3}, 0\\right)\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $\\left(0,0 \\right)$ | $\\left(0, \\pm 2\\sqrt 2 \\right)$ | $\\left(\\pm \\dfrac{4\\sqrt 3}{3}, 0 \\right)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $-8$ | $16$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at\n\n$$\n\\left(0, \\pm 2\\sqrt 2 \\right)\n$$\n\nand its minimum value is $-8$." 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198247 1069 "e-8691" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198247 1071 "Using the method of Lagrange multipliers, find the maximum value of the sum of cosines of the angle measures of a triangle, given that all three angles are acute." 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198247 1072 #uuid "9235c493-f092-5f14-b989-be3b4548a90d" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198247 1074 "Let $x$, $y$, and $z$ be the measures of the angles. We wish to maximize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = \\cos{x}+\\cos{y}+\\cos{z}\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint\n\n$$\nx+y+z=\\pi\n$$\n\nSince the angles are acute, we also have the constraints\n\n$$\n0 \\lt x,y,z \\lt \\dfrac\\pi 2\n$$\n\nLet\n\n$$\ng(x,y,z) = x+y+z\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y,z) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y,z), \\qquad g(x,y,z) = \\pi\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇f(x, y, z) &= λ∇g(x, y, z) \\\\\n⟨f_{x}(x, y, z), f_{y}(x, y, z), f_{z}(x, y, z)⟩ &= λ⟨g_{x}(x, y, z), g_{y}(x, y, z), g_{z}(x, y, z)⟩ \\\\\n⟨ - \\sin x,-\\sin y,-\\sin z⟩ &= λ⟨1, 1, 1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{-\\sin x = λ; - \\sin y = λ; - \\sin z = λ\n$$\n\nFrom the first and second equations above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin x &= \\sin y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the sine function is one-to-one on the interval\n\n$$\n\\left(0, \\dfrac \\pi 2\\right)\n$$\n\nwe can conclude that $x = y$.\n\nSimilarly, from the second and third equations, we get that $z=y =x$. So, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + y + z &= π \\\\\nx + x + x &= π \\\\\n3x &= π \\\\\nx &= \\frac{π}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is reached at the point\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3},\\dfrac{\\pi}{3},\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)\n$$\n\nand the maximum value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(\\frac{π}{3}, \\frac{π}{3}, \\frac{π}{3}) &= \\cos (\\frac{π}{3}) + \\cos (\\frac{π}{3}) + \\cos (\\frac{π}{3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt's easy to show that this point is indeed the maximum and not the minimum. To do that, we can compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(\\frac{π}{3}, \\frac{π}{4}, \\frac{5π}{12}) &= \\cos (\\frac{π}{3}) + \\cos (\\frac{π}{4}) + \\cos (\\frac{5π}{12}) \\\\\n&\\approx 1.47 \\\\\n&< f(\\frac{π}{3}, \\frac{π}{3}, \\frac{π}{3})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198248 1069 "e-9575" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198248 1071 "Given the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = 4y(x^2+y^2)\\,\\mathbf{i} - 4x(x^2+y^2)\\,\\mathbf{j}$, which of the following statements must be true?\n\n1. $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F= 0$\n2. $\\mathbf{F}$ is a source-free vector field\n3. For two arbitrary curves $C_1$ and $C_2$, we have $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s \\neq \\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s$." 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198248 1072 #uuid "366c826f-6f15-5a21-9eaa-d74b6e4d8833" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198248 1074 "If\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nis a vector field with simply connected domain $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2$, then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $\\mathbf{F}$ is a source-free vector field.\n- $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F = \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} = 0$.\n- The flux $\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s}$ along any closed curve $C \\in D$ is zero.\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves $C_1,C_2 \\in D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n$\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s = \\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s$.\n- There exists a stream function $\\psi(x,y)$ for $\\mathbf{F}$.\n\nNote that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)$ is a vector field with the simply connected domain\n\n$$\nD=\\mathbb{R}^2\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y) = 4y(x^2+y^2),\\qquad Q(x,y) =-4x(x^2+y^2)\n$$\n\nComputing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x} = 8xy, \\frac{∂Q}{∂y} &= -8xy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have that\n$\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} = 8xy + (-8xy) = 0$.\n- Statement II is true. This follows from statement I.\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\mathbf F$ is source-free, we have that $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s = \\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s$ for all curves *with the same start and endpoints*.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198249 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048594 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198249 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050910 131